# How I Became Christian (Part 2)
## My journey from existential uncertainty to belief and baptism

*Read part one [here](HOME_URL_PHhow-i-became-christian-1/).*

By the time I turned fifty, I was a solid fifteen years into a growing existential unease. My problems hadn't changed since childhood; it was the optimism that was fading. Like pounding a square peg into a round hole, I finally amassed so much data that I admitted defeat: this isn't how things work.

The defeat I was facing was this: people aren't fundamentally good. History is not a steady upward march toward justice for all. Humanity is trashing the planet faster than every attempt to heal it, and lord help the universe if we ever make it into space. I'm never going to bridge that last gap between me and everyone else, never going to feel truly seen, heard, understood. 

All this churned beneath the surface for years, gaining data points and momentum, until one day it slammed full force into broad mindful daylight. This is what hit me: 

*If what I believe is true&mdash;there is no purpose behind the universe, it just poofed out of the quantum vacuum and everything since then has been forces or waves-slash-particles or superstrings roiling around and that's it&mdash;then my deep sense of ethics is a mirage. There is no right and wrong, no good and evil. There is no love. These are all delusions, and I'm suicidally stupid every time I disadvantage myself to help someone else because it's the 'right' thing to do.*

For the first time in my life, I was seeing the implications of my professed worldview&mdash;and seeing that I did not live by it. I talked a good materialist game in the world of ideas, but where the rubber met the road, I lived by another worldview entirely: one in which there is objective right and wrong, in which love is the highest virtue, in which doing the right thing matters. I didn't always do the right thing, mind you; that much is obvious if you've read this far. But at those times I still *knew* what the right thing would be. This sense of failing to meet a moral standard implied that moral standard existed independently of me. Yet I claimed to believe a worldview that said there was no such thing.

There was no unseeing it: I'd been an accidental hypocrite for fifty years. I would not deliberately remain one. Was I right to believe that moral truth is a feature of the universe, not a human invention? Then my talk needed to align with my walk. Was I deluded to live that way? Then, however starkly barren it sounded, my walk needed to align with my talk. But which belief was true? Is there objective right and wrong, or is there nothing but value-free mechanics?

I tried to start at the beginning and reason my way to an answer, but almost instantly I was reduced to a Descartian impasse: the only thing I knew for sure was that I was here, asking these questions. For months I read arguments for and against materialism and got nowhere. [The logic on both sides was sound, yet it produced opposite conclusions. And then, at last, I saw why.](HOME_URL_PHon-the-use-and-abuse-of-logic/) Like arithmetic, you can use the same valid rules to arrive at different answers by choosing different input. And worldviews are inputs, not outputs. All the arguments I'd been reading for and against materialism ultimately boiled down to *if these foundational assumptions I'm using because I am a materialist and they are compatible with materialism are true, then materialism is true* (or the equivalent for non-materialism). 

It was as simple as simply choosing which one to keep. And that was a no-brainer. I was absolutely, utterly certain there was objective right and wrong, and I was absolutely, utterly certain that this was actual knowledge, though it was not scientific. Materialism had to go. Now I could start figuring out what the existence of objective morality implied about the world.

<details>
<summary>Don't like my argument?
</summary>
That's okay. I'm not trying to convince you. I'm telling you why *I* became Christian. You're free to start from different assumptions, and if you do, you'll reach different conclusions. The only example I urge you to follow is to examine what you believe, and not to believe contradictory things.

For those interested, the logic goes like this:

If materialism, then not objective morality.
If materialism, then only scientific knowledge.

The contrapositives are logically equivalent:

If objective morality, then not materialism.
If knowledge other than scientific, then not materialism.
</details>

Around this time, two Jehovah's Witnesses showed up at my door. I practically dragged them inside. To say I was ready to hear more about Yahweh is an understatement. It was entirely possible the God of the Abrahamic religions wouldn't line up with my knowledge of objective morality, but it was the obvious place to start. I still remembered Dr. Brooks and my former neighbors.

For two years I weekly peppered a wonderful, lovely woman with intense philosophical questions. She answered what she could and gamely researched what she couldn't. Of course I was getting the JW interpretation, but the JW interpretation is, it turns out, surprisingly levelheaded. (I knew too little to even ask the question at this point; I'd only realize it much later.) So I was getting a Biblically solid view of God's character, the nature of Jesus' sacrifice, the future promise, and so on. It all seemed wacko to me at the time, though. Remember, I'd been an academically trained materialist for decades. 

I bought a dozen books by sane people who ended up believing in God without melting their brains: Jennifer Fulwiler, Holly Ordway, Anthony Flew, Francis Collins, CS Lewis, you name it. I bought a dozen Bible translations to make sure I wasn't getting too much bias in any one direction: the NRSV, NIV, Message, KJV, NLT, Orthodox, you name it. I bought a hundred theological books by dozens of scholars: NT Wright, CJH Wright, CS Lewis, Peter Enns, Bart Ehrman, you name it. I read tons about the culture and practices of the Ancient Near East. I read a zillion blog posts and articles and watched a zillion YouTube videos from people all over the Christian spectrum (and outside it). Somewhere in the center of that massive Venn diagram, the shape of Yahweh's will started taking shape for me. 

Little by little, misconceptions peeled away. I'd always thought the Old Testament was the Jewish record of how much more awesome they were than everyone else and that's why God singled them out; turns out it's the record of a fractious, disobedient people who sin left and right yet God keeps loving them, shaping them, inviting them to model something better for the world. I'd always thought the apostles were twelve distinguished gents who were skilled and successful enough to earn a seat near God's own Messiah; turns out they were slow-witted, ineloquent nobodies Christ chose for the strength of their hearts. And so on, and so on, and so on. 

I read other scriptures, too: the Qur'an, the Bhagavad Gita, the Dao De Jing, you name it. I read the philosophers. I was looking for a thread that runs through all serious human thought on the nature of existence: for another of my foundational assumptions is that, being transcendent, this objective morality will be addressed in all places and times. (Arguments against it also count, for arguments only address phenomena that need explaining. Note that even materialism concedes that humans *behave* as if we share a common morality, then explains it as a neurochemical side effect that's proven useful to species survival.)  

I found that thread, and in the many contradictions among traditions, I started to gain a sense for what was transcendent and what was human conjecture. Because I was evaluating the truth of Christianity, I asked questions that distinguished it from other traditions. Was Jesus an actual person who lived? Was he the Jewish Messiah? Did he really die? Do we reincarnate or do we just get the one life? Are we ultimately just part of one divine mind or do we have real, independent selves? It was a lot to work through, and obviously I couldn't definitively answer any of it. I could at least consult the historical evidence for questions about Jesus' existence and death, but answers to the rest were outside the realm of science altogether. All I could do was think about what seemed most likely to me, and what Christianity was saying.

About two years in, I had occasion to speak with Dr. Brooks again. He suggested I read the whole books of Luke and Acts in a single go, and report back on who Jesus seemed to be. This task forced me to ingest the bigger picture instead of analyzing verses and words using commentaries and lexicons, and I finally saw the one thing that distinguished Christianity from all other traditions: it was not a merit-based system. This was mind-blowing.

Back in my atheist days, I'd assumed Jesus had healed people as follows:

&mdash;Please, sir, heal me of this terrible disease.  
&mdash;Do you promise to stop being bad?  
&mdash;I do.  
&mdash;Then I'll heal you. But I warn you, if you do bad stuff again, the disease will come back.

This is not what Jesus did. What Jesus did is heal everyone who asked him. He healed them and then told them God forgave them for all they'd ever done wrong. Then he said goodbye and walked on.

Jesus was not conducting transactions. He was offering God's total, unconditional love. You had to ask him, but you didn't have to earn it or deserve it or promise anything.

If your only exposure to Jesus is cultural, this is hard to see. I'd certainly never seen it until I read Luke and Acts in one go. Frankly, most Christians have never seen it. Our world works on merit-based transactions for *everything*. What you get in life depends on what you do. It's so ingrained we have dozens of ways to express it: *no such thing as a free lunch*, *you get what you deserve*, *serves you right*, *just deserts*, *karma*. Surely the Kingdom of God works the same way. How else could it possibly work? 

Luke-and-Acts-in-one-go showed me how else it could work. It was nothing like the way of the world. It was counterintuitive, crazy, unfathomable, unlike any other tradition. As I read more of the Bible, I saw that this radical forgiveness had always been God's plan; now Jesus was telling us the moment had arrived.

I saw it now, why Dr. Brooks and my old neighbors had been such incongruous data points. They were the rare Christian who'd really understood the gospel.

This was it. I had found pure, utter goodness. And in that instant I *knew* he was real. I still had ninety thousand questions, but I could trust him when he said he was making all things right&mdash;which is the narrative arc of the whole Bible. I could trust that man's thousand thousand years of death and cruelty and disease were part of God's good plan, though I utterly did not see how. Of course I didn't see how; I am a Flatland line to God's infinite-dimensional sphere. I now saw that I was not being asked to understand everything. I was being asked to accept God's radical offer of love based on what I did understand. 

I found a local mainline church and got baptized that August. The poor Jehovah's Witness was crestfallen that after all her tutoring I'd strayed into 'false religion', but I was grateful to her. This was far from the end of my journey, though. I knew enough to take the leap and trust God; but I knew there were important things I didn't understand, things I needed to know about what God did and did not expect from me. The next seven years would be an intense and often harrowing period of finding God's truth amid all the distortions of human doctrine.

